= Meaning representation =

== Overview ==
A '''meaning representation''' is a formal or structured representation intended to encode aspects of the meaning or content of linguistic expressions.

Meaning representations make selected semantic information explicit so that it can be interpreted, compared, queried, reasoned over, or processed computationally.

No representation is a neutral transcription of “the meaning itself.” Every formalism chooses what distinctions to encode and what to omit.

== Why represent meaning? ==
Natural-language strings can be ambiguous and structurally variable.

A meaning representation can normalize selected distinctions across surface forms.

For example:

<blockquote>Ada wrote the program.</blockquote>

and

<blockquote>The program was written by Ada.</blockquote>

differ syntactically but can share an underlying event relation in a semantic representation.

== What can be represented? ==
Depending on the formalism, a representation may encode:

* entities and variables;
* predicates and relations;
* events and participants;
* quantification;
* negation;
* tense and aspect;
* modality;
* reference;
* semantic roles;
* discourse relations;
* scope;
* presupposition or discourse state.

No single system necessarily represents all of these.

== Logical forms ==
Formal semantics can translate linguistic expressions into logical representations.

A simplified example:

<pre>
∃x (program(x) ∧ wrote(Ada,x))
</pre>

Logical forms support explicit inference when their semantics is defined, but they require decisions about ontology, scope, reference, and granularity.

== Graph representations ==
Some computational formalisms represent meaning as graphs.

Nodes can represent entities, events, or concepts; edges can represent semantic roles or relations.

Abstract Meaning Representation (AMR) is one prominent graph-based framework, but it is one representation scheme rather than a universal semantic standard.

== Discourse representations ==
Discourse Representation Theory and dynamic approaches represent discourse referents and conditions across multiple sentences, supporting phenomena such as anaphora and quantification.

This differs from a sentence-level predicate graph.

== Semantic parsing ==
'''Semantic parsing''' maps language into a target meaning representation.

Its evaluation therefore depends on the target formalism. A parser can score highly against annotations while the representation itself still omits pragmatic, contextual, or world knowledge.

== Relation to knowledge representation ==
[[Knowledge representation]] models knowledge about a domain or world.

Meaning representation models the interpreted content of linguistic expressions.

The two can connect—for example, a semantic parser can map an utterance into a knowledge-base query—but they are not the same problem.

== Relation to embeddings ==
Learned vectors can encode semantic information, but a dense embedding is not automatically a symbolic meaning representation.

A symbolic formalism usually exposes named structure and relations; an embedding distributes information across numeric dimensions.

Hybrid systems can use both.

== Epistemic notes ==
Meaning representations are task- and theory-dependent.

Different representations can preserve different aspects of the same utterance. Inter-annotator disagreement, parser error, underspecification, and ontology mismatch are normal engineering and scientific concerns.

